  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nDry weather continues for the Quad State region today as surface\nhigh pressure remains in the east. An elongated area of ridging\nacross much of the southern half of the country has allowed for\ntemperatures to reach the 90s across the area. Heat index values\nare presently in the mid-90s in the north and near 100 in the\nsouth. Patchy cumulus has formed, mainly in the eastern half of\nthe Quad State.\n\nSoutherly winds today remain and increase late tonight out of\nthe SSW, with winds rising to 10-15mph tomorrow. The southerly\nflow will advect moisture into the region from the Lower\nMississippi via Arkansas/Tennessee, and keep low temperatures in\nthe low to mid 70s. This warm and moist advection will raise\ntemperatures and heat indices tomorrow. While the NBM\ninitialization may be a bit overzealous on moisture advection,\nand T/Td were lowered slightly in collaboration with western\nneighbors as a result, highs in the mid-90s to upper 90s remain\nforecast for most of the Quad State, with 100 degrees most\nlikely in the Ozarks. Heat indices of 102-108 are forecast\n(highest in the southwest, lowest in the Kentucky Pennyrile),\nand a heat advisory has been issued for the entire Quad State\nregion. HeatRisk is level 3 for tomorrow and performed well for\nthe recent hot/dry extreme heat event. Since rain has been \ndifficult to come by of late, this should remain the case for\ntomorrow.\n\nHowever, showers and thunderstorms do enter the picture\ntomorrow, with some models developing a WSW to ENE oriented\nbroken line of convection midday. Others hold off convective\nactivity until later in the afternoon. Any areas which receive\nmidday storms may fall short of heat advisory criteria, and\nafternoon storms will provide heat relief for those that receive\nthem. Reasonable CAPE and decent lapse rates will be offset by\nlimited shear. DCAPE values over 1000J/kg suggest the primary\nstrong to severe weather hazard will be straight-line winds from\ndownbursts. A marginal risk for severe weather covers\napproximately the northern half of the Quad State region. Shower\nand storm activity increases later in the evening and remains\noff and on through the night and much of Thursday, but severe\nweather potential drops off Wednesday evening.\n\nA cold frontal passage Thursday will contribute to highs in the\n80s Thursday and Friday, but models project the boundary to lift\nback north Saturday. Lower end precipitation chances remain\nFriday through Sunday, generally around 20-30%. Sources for\nshowers/storms include moisture streaming near the boundary \nFriday into Saturday on the southern edge of the Quad State and\na weakening system approaching from the northwest during the\nweekend. Limited precipitation potential remains early next week\nas high pressure sits over the Mid-Atlantic and activity is most\nprevalent near the Great Lakes. With ridging slowly building\nlate weekend and lingering to early next week, more highs in the\nlower 90s are forecast, though conditions should stay below\nAdvisory level criteria.",
